Derive flotation recovery models and test what their parameters can explain (@mine-flotation-kinetics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mining-and-mineral-engineering, #source "../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA", #source "../sources/Wills-Mineral-Processing-Technology.lingua#r_02W7VCEB3274XY5AKSH9SGPV1A", #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Mineral-Processing-NPTEL.lingua#r_5VQ67PA1160RQ1EWN17211XBWD", #mining-and-mineral-engineering "Roughers-cleaners-scavengers-and-recycle.lingua#r_2BZJNDFG57WRXSJX0GNMYG86DX") { r_0FGGNJEAT08S8WDCECHNVJBNS0
**Read:** [Tratamento de Minérios — CETEM, sixth edition](../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA), the complete flotation theory and kinetics discussions; [Wills’ Mineral Processing Technology — Wills and Finch, eighth edition](../sources/Wills-Mineral-Processing-Technology.lingua#r_02W7VCEB3274XY5AKSH9SGPV1A), chapter 12 in full and appendices V–VI, when available; [Introduction to Mineral Processing — Arun Kumar Majumder, NPTEL](../sources/Introduction-to-Mineral-Processing-NPTEL.lingua#r_5VQ67PA1160RQ1EWN17211XBWD), the complete flotation lecture sequence.

Begin with a recoverable constituent class and a first-order disappearance model. A batch test gives recovery R(t) = R∞(1 − exp(−kt)), while an ideal continuously stirred tank at steady state gives R = R∞ kτ/(1 + kτ). Here R∞ is a class-specific limiting fraction, k is a rate constant, and τ is mean residence time on a consistent basis. These models share assumptions about the class but differ in residence-time history.

For n identical perfectly mixed tanks in series with total mean time τ, the corresponding result is R = R∞[1 − (1 + kτ/n)^−n]. Derive it from stage constituent balances. With R∞ = 0.9, k = 0.1 min⁻¹, and total τ = 10 min, a single ideal tank gives 0.45 and two equal tanks give 0.50; the ideal batch value at ten minutes is about 0.56891. These are invented model comparisons, not plant predictions.

Real populations have distributed rate behavior, liberation, residence times, froth recovery, and entrainment. A fitted k can absorb several changing mechanisms. Inspect parameter correlation, data-time coverage, residuals, independent validation, and sensitivity to the assumed model before interpreting it physically.

**Work:** fit at least two plausible models to a documented time series, include measurement uncertainty, and validate on independent conditions. Reproduce the ideal limits kτ → 0 and large kτ, compare batch and continuous assumptions, and explain why laboratory recovery cannot simply be assigned to a plant residence time. Preserve original observations, fits, and failure cases.

**Continue:** [Design a flotation circuit through connected streams and competing objectives](Roughers-cleaners-scavengers-and-recycle.lingua#r_2BZJNDFG57WRXSJX0GNMYG86DX).

